  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are systematically too high versus realized prices (roughly +2 to +6 EUR across most horizons). Apply a downward level shift to the whole path.",
    "Downtrend underreaction / lag: when history shows a strong selloff, the model projects stabilization/rebound too early; realized continues drifting lower. Add extra negative drift in the first 1–20 days after a pronounced decline.",
    "Over-optimistic rebounds: local upticks in history lead to forecasted rallies (peaks ~71–74) that do not materialize; realized stays range-bound/lower. Damp rebound amplitude (shrink deviations from last price toward 0).",
    "Horizon-dependent bias: error tends to be largest in the mid-horizon (~days 10–25), where the model remains elevated while realized is still depressed. Use a larger downward correction in days 10–25 than in days 1–5 or 26–30.",
    "Insufficient mean reversion downward: forecasts revert toward a higher long-run mean than the market regime supports (realized mean ~64–67). Re-anchor long-run mean lower (regime shift) and cap forecasts above ~70 unless strong upward momentum persists."
  ]
}
